From: GoProSlowYo <68455785+goproslowyo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 21:25:09 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 03/22] dndcp: Keep the faked pointer on screen, never leave
 its button down

XOpenDisplay() can return NULL, and the uinput setup used the result
without checking. The daemon then crashed while registering drag and
drop instead of falling back to XTest. This happens with a compositor
that starts Xwayland on demand and stops it when the last client exits,
as sway does.

A press is faked through uinput to start a host to guest drag, and the
host releases it when it drops. If the drag never starts there is no
drop and no release. A button left down holds the pointer in a drag for
the rest of the session, and to the user the desktop looks frozen.
Release it when the UI is reset, where the host last had the pointer,
without moving the detection window. The X server's idea of the pointer
is stale while it is over a Wayland surface, and the host can still be
dragging. The reset also runs from the destructor, which has already
deleted the detection window, so the release has to work without one.

The host's coordinates go to the uinput device unchecked. Clamp them in
FakeMouse_Move(), which every uinput move goes through, to the range the
device was declared with. The X server keeps a warp or an XTest motion
on the screen by itself.

That range is the screen size when the device was created. After a
resolution change the compositor scales the old range onto the new
output, and the clamp cuts off coordinates beyond the old size. The
device is now recreated when the screen size has changed, checked at the
start of each drag in either direction, as the GTK3 build already does.

Set INPUT_PROP_DIRECT and INPUT_PROP_POINTER on the uinput device.
Without a property the kernel also exposes the device as a joystick, and
libinput misclassifies it, which has broken the side buttons of a real
mouse.
